<body>
<main data-frwd-document="">
<article data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000001" class="paper">

<header data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000002">
<h1 data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000003" class="title">Saturation kinetics of thin-film humidity sensors</h1>
<p data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000004" class="authors">R. Okonkwo, M. Halvorsen</p>
<p data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000005" class="affiliation">Northwind Instruments, Materials Group</p>
</header>

<section data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000006" class="abstract">
<h2 data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000007">Abstract</h2>
<p data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000008">Thin-film humidity sensors are usually modelled as first-order systems, which fits their early response and fails badly near saturation. We fit a two-term model to eleven devices and find that the second-order desorption term accounts for the residual error that first-order fits attribute to drift.</p>
</section>

<section data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000009">
<h2 data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000010">Model</h2>
<p data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000011">Let <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>θ</mi></math> be fractional saturation and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>τ</mi></math> dimensionless time. The first-order model treats adsorption as proportional to the unoccupied fraction alone. Adding a desorption term quadratic in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>θ</mi></math> gives <a data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000012" href="#eq-kinetics">equation (1)</a>:</p>

<div data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000013" class="equation" id="eq-kinetics">
<math data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000014" display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
<mrow>
<mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac>
<mo>=</mo>
<mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow>
<mo>−</mo>
<msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup>
</mrow>
</math>
</div>

<p data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000015">The equation is MathML, not an image of an equation. It reflows, it scales with the text, a screen reader can speak it, and a tool that has never heard of FRWD can still extract it as structure rather than pixels.</p>
</section>

<section data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000016">
<h2 data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000017">Results</h2>
<p data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000018">Saturation rises steeply through the first five time constants and then flattens, as shown in <a data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000019" href="#fig-saturation">Figure 1</a>. Fitted constants for the three film thicknesses appear in <a data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000020" href="#tab-constants">Table 1</a>.</p>

<figure data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000021" id="fig-saturation">
<svg aria-labelledby="plot-title" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" role="img" style="color: var(--accent)" viewBox="0 0 640 290"><title id="plot-title">Saturation rises steeply to about 0.66 by τ = 5 and flattens towards 0.95 by τ = 10.</title><line stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.13" x1="46" x2="624" y1="214.0" y2="214.0"></line><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" x="38" y="218.0">0.00</text><line stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.13" x1="46" x2="624" y1="165.0" y2="165.0"></line><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" x="38" y="169.0">0.25</text><line stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.13" x1="46" x2="624" y1="116.0" y2="116.0"></line><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" x="38" y="120.0">0.50</text><line stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.13" x1="46" x2="624" y1="67.0" y2="67.0"></line><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" x="38" y="71.0">0.75</text><line stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.13" x1="46" x2="624" y1="18.0" y2="18.0"></line><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" x="38" y="22.0">1.00</text><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" x="46.0" y="232">0</text><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" x="161.6" y="232">2</text><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" x="277.2" y="232">4</text><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" x="392.8" y="232">6</text><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" x="508.4" y="232">8</text><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" x="624.0" y="232">10</text><path d="M46.0 210.1 L103.8 196.4 L161.6 172.8 L219.4 143.4 L277.2 112.1 L335.0 84.6 L392.8 63.1 L450.6 47.4 L508.4 37.6 L566.2 31.7 L624.0 27.8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.2"></path><circle cx="46.0" cy="210.1" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="103.8" cy="196.4" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="161.6" cy="172.8" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="219.4" cy="143.4" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="277.2" cy="112.1" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="335.0" cy="84.6" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="392.8" cy="63.1" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="450.6" cy="47.4" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="508.4" cy="37.6" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="566.2" cy="31.7" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><circle cx="624.0" cy="27.8" fill="currentColor" r="3"></circle><line stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.45" x1="46" x2="624" y1="214" y2="214"></line><text fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.65" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" x="335" y="252">dimensionless time τ</text></svg>
<figcaption data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000022">Mean fractional saturation against dimensionless time for eleven devices at 220 nm film thickness. The curve is the two-term fit; markers are measured means. Error bars are omitted at this scale because they are smaller than the markers.</figcaption>
</figure>

<div data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000023" class="table-scroll">
<table data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000024" id="tab-constants">
<caption data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000025">Fitted rate constants by film thickness, with residual error under each model.</caption>
<thead>
<tr><th scope="col">Thickness (nm)</th><th scope="col">k</th><th scope="col">k₂</th><th scope="col">RMSE, first-order</th><th scope="col">RMSE, two-term</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">140</th><td>0.42</td><td>0.11</td><td>0.038</td><td>0.009</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">220</th><td>0.31</td><td>0.14</td><td>0.041</td><td>0.007</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">310</th><td>0.24</td><td>0.19</td><td>0.052</td><td>0.011</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>

<section data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000026">
<h2 data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000027">Discussion</h2>
<p data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000028">The residual that first-order fits attribute to sensor drift is, on this evidence, the desorption term. That distinction matters in service: drift is corrected by recalibration, whereas a systematic model error is not, and a device recalibrated against the wrong model will read confidently and incorrectly near saturation.</p>
<p data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000029">The effect grows with film thickness, which is consistent with desorption being limited by diffusion out of the film rather than by surface kinetics.</p>
</section>

<section data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000030">
<h2 data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000031">References</h2>
<ol data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000032" class="references">
<li data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000033"><cite>Adsorption dynamics in polymer humidity sensors</cite>. Journal of Sensor Physics, 2024.</li>
<li data-frwd-id="5e2c8a7f-4d31-4b96-8c05-000000000034"><cite>Calibration practice for field-deployed hygrometry</cite>. Northwind Technical Note 11, 2025.</li>
</ol>
</section>

</article>
</main>

</body></html>